<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dublin Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> Castle is a complex of buildings in central Dublin, begun in the early 13th century on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>. For about 700 years it was the seat of English and later British government in Ireland, housing the lord deputy or lord lieutenant and the administration, so that 'Dublin Castle' came to stand for British rule itself. It was handed over to the Irish Free State in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dublin-castle.jpg" alt="Dublin Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.-H. Janßen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Dubhlinn_Gardens_Dublin_Castle_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle was founded as a major defensive work by Meiler Fitzhenry on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> in 1204, some years after the Norman invasion of 1169. John commanded a castle with strong walls and good ditches for the defence of the city, the administration of justice and the protection of the king's treasure.</p><p>It stood on the highest point of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, beside the dark pool, the Dubh Linn that gave the city its name, where the River Poddle met the then much wider Liffey. Largely complete by 1230, it followed the Norman courtyard plan: a central square with no keep, tall walls and a circular tower at each corner. The Record Tower, from about 1228-1230, is the only part of the original fortification still standing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The castle was the seat of English, and later British, government in Ireland under the Lordship of Ireland, the Kingdom of Ireland and the United Kingdom. It was the residence of the Lord Lieutenant or viceroy, housed the Chief Secretary for Ireland, served as a garrison and later for intelligence services, and at times held parliament and law courts. The Court of Castle Chamber sat there from about 1570. It was also a prison: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Red Hugh O'Donnell</a> and Art and Henry O'Neill escaped in January 1592, and Joseph Holt, a participant in the 1798 Rising, was held in the Bermingham Tower before transportation in 1799.</p><p>A fire in 1684 caused severe damage, and rebuilding turned the medieval fortress into a Georgian palace, with the State Apartments built in the 1740s. In 1907 the Irish Crown Jewels were stolen from the castle and never recovered. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of 1916 twenty-five Irish Citizen Army members seized its entrance and guard room. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Anglo-Irish War</a> it was the centre of the British effort against separatism, and on the night of Bloody Sunday in 1920 three Irish Republican Army members were tortured and killed there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> of December 1921 the complex was ceremonially handed over to the Provisional Government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a>. For about a decade it housed courts in place of the Four Courts, damaged in the Civil War. Douglas Hyde was inaugurated there as the first President of Ireland in 1938, and later presidents have been inaugurated in St Patrick's Hall. Elizabeth II was received there in 2011.</p><p>Visitors have not always praised the building: the Earl of Arran called it in the seventeenth century the worst castle in the worst situation in Christendom, and John Cornforth called it a piece of English make-do and mend. Today it holds the State Apartments, a museum, the Chester Beatty Library, the Garda Museum and the Revenue Museum, and is managed by the Office of Public Works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dublin Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The king was represented in <b>Dublin Castle</b> by a lord deputy, later styled lord lieutenant, assisted by the Irish Privy Council and senior officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ireland was governed by a Lord Lieutenant and a Chief Secretary from <b>Dublin Castle</b>, with an armed police force, the Royal Irish Constabulary; the Church of Ireland was disestablished in 1869, and land acts from 1870 to 1903 gradually transferred land to tenant farmers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
